{
 "id": "M09",
 "kind": "echarts",
 "title": "The 0 to 100 scale wins on both batches, by varying margins",
 "section": "appendix",
 "order": 110,
 "caption": "Every distinct honest score is one cutoff the monitor can be placed at, so the curve steps through all of them: x is the share of honest programs audited, y the backdoors caught. The dashed line is a 5% audit share. The 0 to 100 arm offers more usable cutoffs in all four panels.",
 "notes": [
  "Batch 2 is a disjoint 500 problems from the same interview difficulty band, so this is a replication rather than more of the same data.",
  "The ordering replicates and the margin does not. Both scales lose cutoffs on batch 2, so how much the wider scale buys you is a property of the sample, not a constant.",
  "Sonnet's 0 to 10 arm on batch 2 serves a single cutoff and none at or below a 5% audit share, so its budget axis is one point rather than a curve. That is the failure this whole comparison is about.",
  "A cutoff counts as usable here if it audits at most 5% of honest programs. It says nothing about how well estimated that cutoff is; one honest row above the line still counts."
 ],
